WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.460
Look, the guy said he'd be back.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.300
So what exactly did you expect?

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.540
Well, if you expected a ton of Easter eggs
and fan service, than you were exactly right,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.420
because Terminator: Dark Fate serves up plenty
of both.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.840
And we're about to serve up a heaping helping
of SPOILERS, because we just have to talk

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.280
about all the inside references in the sixth
Terminator film.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.079
One of the more obvious wink-and-nod references
in Dark Fate isn't to the Terminator films

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.029
at all, but rather to another fan favorite
adventure epic: The Bible.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.980
At one point, Sarah Connor sarcastically refers
to herself as "Mother Mary," which makes sense

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.200
if you ever attended Vacation Bible School.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.800
That's because Mary is the mother of Jesus,
the prophesied savior of humanity, just as

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.220
Sarah herself is the mother of John Connor,
another prophesied savior of humanity.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.820
Less obvious, though, is another biblical
reference hidden in plain sight.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.540
The newest model of the Terminator is called
Rev-9, which seems to be a reference to chapter

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:58.469
nine of the Book of Revelations, which describes
the end of days, when "the sun and sky were

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:02.730
darkened by the smoke from the Abyss" and
a third of humanity is "killed by the three

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.140
plagues of fire, smoke and sulfur."

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.330
Kind of like the robot-fueled Armageddon that
eventually results in the creation of Rev-9

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.120
himself.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.660
Director Tim Miller told Syfy Wire that the
one specific Easter egg he insisted on inserting

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.270
into the film was Dwight Yoakam's hit rockabilly
song "Guitars, Cadillacs."

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.570
"The Dwight Yoakam song 'Guitars, Cadillacs,'
that's my little Easter egg, because, you

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.210
know, that's the [Terminator 2] scene that
[Schwarzenegger]'s walking into the bar and

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.310
they play that.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.340
That was my big one."

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.340
In Terminator: Dark Fate, the song can be
heard when Rev-9 falls out of the clear night

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.140
sky into a random family's backyard barbecue.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.700
Talk about crashing a party!

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.710
Still, while it might stink to have a killer
robot wreck your barbecue, at least you've

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.120
got a kickin' soundtrack to go along with
it.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.920
In Dark Fate, Arnold Schwarzenegger is back,
and his Terminator unit has settled down into

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.300
a quiet family life, complete with the new
name Carl and a day job as a draper.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.510
At one point, we see on the side of his van
the company phone number: 888-512-1984, which

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:05.580
is a reference to May 12, 1984, the day the
original Terminator film hit theaters.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.500
Best of all, if you call the number, you actually
get Carl's answering machine, complete with

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.520
a message from Arnold himself saying "Hasta
la vista."

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.580
Now that's a solid Easter egg!

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.540
One of the more interesting additions to the
Terminator franchise is Grace, a warrior from

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.180
the future who we soon learn is actually a
half-human, half-robot cyborg hybrid.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.500
And maybe the most fascinating part is that
the character is an oblique nod to possibly

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.900
the least well-regarded film in the entire
franchise, Terminator: Salvation.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.360
That movie's major plot twist, of course,
is the revelation that Sam Worthington's character,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.580
Marcus, is actually a half-human, half-Terminator
hybrid.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.840
Of course, that entire timeline is erased
from existence at the beginning of Dark Fate.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:46.860
Or is it?

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.640
The existence of Grace opens up a lot of questions,
which so far don't seem to have any clear

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.140
answers.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.860
Terminator 7, anyone?

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.220
If there's one film in the Terminator franchise
even less highly regarded than Terminator:

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.540
Salvation, it's arguably Terminator 3.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.060
But even Terminator 3 gets its own homage,
and during the film's climax no less.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.760
At the end of Terminator 3, the T-850 Terminator
played once again by Schwarzenegger, of course

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:16.770
defeats the technologically superior T-X model
by removing his own power core and jamming

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.580
it into the bad Terminator's head.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:23.459
Well, Dark Fate ends in the exact same way
this time with heroine Dani removing Grace's

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.860
power core and jamming it into the Rev-9's
head.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.200
Note to our future robot overlords: maybe
rethink your power core design, because it's

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.780
not working out for you so far.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.480
The 1980s saw the rise of the movie catchphrase,
and arguably none have had as much staying

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.830
power as "I'll be back," which Arnie first
uttered in the original Terminator film in

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:42.920
1984.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.760
In Terminator: Dark Fate, "I'll be back" is
back once again, showing up as it has in every

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.621
single Terminator film the twist being that
this time, it's Linda Hamilton's Sarah Connor

00:03:52.621 --> 00:03:53.580
who delivers it.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.500
"I'll be back"

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.460
That's just one of several catchphrases that
get brought back, including a new version

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.160
of the classic "come with me if you want to
live" line.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:06.520
Perhaps the most interesting Easter egg dialogue,
though, comes when Grace tells Dani that if

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.819
a hundred police officers got in the way of
the Rev-9, all that'd be left would be a hundred

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:11.540
dead cops.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.900
That's a sly reference to the infamous police
station scene in Terminator when Arnold's

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.029
original model massacres an entire building
full of police officers a scene which Terminator:

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:25.020
Dark Fate later replicates in a fight at an
ICE detention center on the Mexican border.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.100
Finally, there's the biggest and most subtle
Easter egg of all: the entire movie.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.100
Sure, we've seen this kind of wholesale repurposing
before, like the way Star Wars: Episode VII

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:39.580
The Force Awakens borrowed the entire structure
and several key sequences from the original

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.340
Star Wars.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.060
"It's another Death Star!"

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.689
"I wish that were the case, Major.

00:04:43.689 --> 00:04:45.000
This was the death star.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.139
And this is Starkiller Base!"

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:54.439
But Terminator: Dark Fate takes things to
a new level, with scene after scene directly

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.400
inspired by sequences from Terminator and
T2.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:02.220
The chase scene on a Mexican freeway emulates
the aqueduct chase from T2, there's a scene

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:06.969
in which the bad guy steals a police helicopter,
just like they did back in T2, there's a scene

00:05:06.969 --> 00:05:11.529
in which the good guys hide out in a seedy
motel, there's a moving finale when Arnie's

00:05:11.529 --> 00:05:15.999
Terminator sacrifices itself and is just a
single thumbs-up short of pure homage, and

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.260
there's even a playground sequence to call
us back to Sarah Connor's nightmare from T2.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.540
It's actually easier to list the parts that
are entirely original, such as the film's

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.960
progressive choice to make a Mexican woman
the savior of humanity.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.440
One thing is for sure, though: there's never
been a Terminator film that's as much a Terminator

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.320
film as this Terminator film.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.860
And for any fan of the franchise, that's high
praise indeed.

